# The hook shims are POSIX `sh`, and they must be LF in EVERY checkout —
# not because CRLF is untidy, but because the binary embeds these bytes at
# compile time (`install::SHIM`, an `include_str!`). Nothing here declared a
# line ending, so each checkout obeyed its own `core.autocrlf`, and Git for
# Windows defaults that to true: the v1.14.0 Windows release shipped
# `templates/hooks/pre-commit` with 82 CR bytes, and `amont.exe` carried
# `#!/bin/sh\r\n` embedded — so every `amont install` there wrote hooks with
# a carriage return in the shebang. Git for Windows tolerates it today (its
# CI runs a real commit through those shims), which is exactly why nothing
# caught it: a release artifact that differs by BUILD HOST, and a `\r` in a
# shell script waiting to end up inside a value.
# BOTH copies: the repository's installable set, and the one the crate
# embeds (`include_str!("../templates/hooks/pre-commit")`). A pattern
# containing a slash is anchored to this file's directory, so the plain
# `templates/hooks/*` below does NOT reach the crate's copy — which is
# precisely the file that ends up inside the binary.
templates/hooks/* text eol=lf
crates/amont-runtime/templates/hooks/* text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf

# The installers people pipe into a shell, for the same reason.
install/install.sh text eol=lf
